==English==
[[File:Euro coins and banknotes.jpg|thumb|Euro banknotes and coins.]]

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈjʊəɹəʊ/|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|en-uk-euro.ogg|a=UK}}
* {{IPA|en|/ˈjʊɹoʊ/|/ˈjɝoʊ/|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ʊəɹəʊ|ʊɹoʊ|s=2}}

===Etymology 1===
The name euro was the winner of a contest open to the general public to propose names for the new European currency, and as such is technically a [[neologism]], although it obviously alludes to the common root of geographical names for the continent Europe, derived from {{der|en|la|Europa}}, from {{der|en|grc|Εὐρώπη}}, the name in Greek mythology of a princess, abducted by Zeus as a bull across the Bosporus. According to the official story, the term was {{coin|en|nobycat=1|nocap=1|nat=Belgian|occ=teacher and esperantist|Germain Pirlot|in=1995}}, who suggested it in a letter to {{w|Jacques Santer}}, then President of the European Commission.

====Alternative forms====
* {{l|en|Euro}} {{qualifier|proscribed}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun|s|euro}}

# {{senseid|en|Q4916}}{{lb|en|currencies}} The [[currency]] unit of the {{w|European Monetary Union}}. Symbol: '''[[€]]'''
#: {{syn|en|EUR}}
# A [[coin]] with a [[face value]] of one euro.
# {{abbreviation of|en|European|nodot=1}} in any sense.
#: {{ux|en|'''euro''' size}}
#: {{ux|en|'''euro''' style pad}}

=====Usage notes=====
* The plural form ''euro'' was previously recommended for use in official European documents written in English, but this recommendation has been amended to {{m|en|euros}}. However, in Irish English the plural ''euro'' is now well-established. For further information, see {{w|Language and the euro}} on Wikipedia.

